var tafsirData_67 = {
  "1": "\"Blessed is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority. And He is Most Capable of everything.\" opens Surah al-Mulk with glorification of authority and capability, transitioning from household examples to cosmic wonder.\n\n\"Blessed is the One\" translates “Tabarak,” abundance of exaltation, goodness, and blessing. Blessedness suggests overflowing good, like infinite bounty and excellent provision, not just status. Placing blessing first recalls purging families from Fire and admitting them into Gardens; blessing is the source.\n\n\"in Whose Hands rests all authority\" translates “biyadihi al-mulk,” in His Hands is kingship, dominion, and control. Hands plural indicates grandeur, authority over treasuries of heavens and earth, honour that belongs to Allah and His Messenger, and the descending command between seven heavens. After surahs about divorce limits, oaths, and household intrigue, this reminds that all authority ultimately rests not with spouses, tribes, or traders who left the pulpit for caravan fanfare, but with the One whose Hands hold death and life He created to test.\n\n\"And He is Most Capable of everything\" closes with capability threaded throughout, the One who created death and life, shaped perfectly, expelled fortified peoples without horse or camel, multiplied good loans, and prepared severe and excellent provisions. Capability answers those who thought they could extinguish light with mouths or flee death that will meet them.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read authority as distant autocracy. The next verse says He created death and life to test which is best in deeds, showing authority is exercised for wise testing, not caprice, by the Almighty, All-Forgiving.\n\nFor daily life, remembering blessed authority steadies trust when families falter, when divorce strains resources, or when wealth and children test. Entrust affairs to Hands that hold all authority, act mindfully to best ability, and seek blessing that flows from those Hands alone, not from shields, strongholds, or merchandise that fanfare briefly makes attractive.",
  "2": "\"˹He is the One˺ Who created death and life in order to test which of you is best in deeds. And He is the Almighty, All-Forgiving.\" explains why blessed authority holds everything, linking capability to testing and pairing might with forgiveness.\n\n\"Who created death and life\" orders creation with death before life, highlighting that death is created, appointed without delay when its time comes, not random. Life is created as arena where ears, sight, and intellect are given yet hardly thanked, and where spouses and children test. Creating both shows complete authority, from the drop to the seven heavens layered, from the soul reaching the throat to being returned to the Knower.\n\n\"in order to test which of you is best in deeds\" gives purpose, or “li-yabluwakum ayyukum ahsanu ‘amalan.” Test, or “bala’,” reveals quality, as iron with great might tests who will stand for Him without seeing Him, and as wealth and children test who will not be diverted from remembrance. Best in deeds measures sincerity and excellence, not merely quantity, aligning with the exchange that saves from painful punishment through faith and striving with wealth and lives, and with worship that protects families from Fire. Best captures humble, sincere repentance, making room, guarding chastity, and being true to covenants.\n\n\"And He is the Almighty, All-Forgiving\" pairs power with pardon. Almighty, or “‘Aziz,” ensures test results have meaning, as He can reward immensely and punish severely, can perfect light, and can honour or debase. All-Forgiving, or “Ghafur,” ensures failure in the test is not final if sincere return occurs, as He is Best Provider, Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing, who multiplies good loans and forgives. Might without forgiveness would be despair; forgiveness without might would be wish. Together they define blessed authority that both tests rigorously and forgives generously.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat life as mere exam to fear, forgetting creation for purpose and with mercy, like smoothing earth to eat from provision and adornment of lamps for guidance.\n\nFor practice, view each day as test question. Answer with deed best in sight of the Almighty who sees what you do and who forgives what you repent, turning wealth, family, and Friday gathering into opportunities to deliver best deeds under Hands that hold all authority and are Most Capable of rewarding them eternally.",
  "3": "\"˹He is the One˺ Who created seven heavens, one above the other. You will never see any imperfection in the creation of the Most Compassionate. So look again: do you see any flaws?\" directs eyes upward after stating blessed authority, death and life creation, and test of best deeds. The verse uses cosmic perfection to reaffirm capability and mercy before discussing warnings about earth swallowing or stones.\n\n\"Who created seven heavens, one above the other\" recalls the seven heavens in layers also mentioned as the divine command descends between them, now inviting visual verification. One above the other, or “tibaqan,” suggests consistent stacking without support you perceive, inviting repeated looking as later commanded to look again and again until sight returns frustrated and weary without finding crack. The number seven indicates completeness, echoing seven heavens and likewise for the earth, showing layered grandeur that mirrors layered family limits and layered community ranks that the Quran organizes.\n\n\"You will never see any imperfection in the creation of the Most Compassionate\" names the creator as “al-Rahman,” the Most Compassionate, the same who smoothed earth for you to eat, holds birds, and provides flowing water that could sink. Imperfection here is “futur,” crack, fissure, or incoherence. The claim is that you will never see flaw, not because you have not looked carefully, but because none exists in His making, whether in the heavens’ expanse or in the womb where He shaped you perfecting form.\n\n\"So look again: do you see any flaws?\" issues a challenge to verify. The double looking invites both the denier who said “ancient fables” when revelations were recited and the humble believer who seeks proof that Light is true. Looking again is not casual glance, but attentive seeking akin to hearing and obeying, counting waiting periods accurately, and calling two reliable men to witness. The question expects honest answer. No flaw will be seen, just as no flaw will be found in balance sent for justice when upheld.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this denies apparent disturbances like eclipses or storms of stones. Those are not flaws but purposeful signs and warnings within a flawless system, as with torment that reduces gardens to ashes after neglect.\n\nFor practice, use the challenge literally. Raise eyes to heavens, seek flaws, find none, and let that certainty strengthen trust in the Most Compassionate who also governs household limits, provision from unimaginable sources, and ease after hardship, knowing the flawless Creator of layered heavens can also perfect your light and forgive when you turn sincerely, inviting you to walk upright on the Straight Path rather than crawl facedown.",
  "4": "\"Then look again and again— your sight will return frustrated and weary.\" continues the challenge after stating you will never see any imperfection in the creation of the Most Compassionate and asking do you see any flaws. The verse urges repeated, careful inspection.\n\n“Then look again and again” translates “thumma irji‘ al-basara karratayni,” return your sight time after time, not merely a quick glance. The repetition emphasizes thoroughness, as with counting waiting periods accurately, calling two reliable witnesses, or making room when told repeatedly across gatherings. Gaze should be returned, focused, and allowed to search for cracks in the seven heavens layered one above another, for flaws in the smooth earth prepared for you, for breaks in the birds’ flight held by the Most Compassionate.\n\n“your sight will return frustrated and weary” describes the result. “Frustrated” or “khasi’an” is humbled, driven back, unable to find flaw; “weary” or “hasir” is exhausted from effort without success. The Most Compassionate’s creation is flawless not because inspection is discouraged, but because inspection, however long, cannot find incoherence. This mirrors earlier tests where those who deviated after knowing the messenger found their hearts caused to deviate, and where hardened hearts after long time found no understanding. Honest looking returns weary, dishonest looking pretends to find flaws that are not there, like calling clear revelations ancient fables.\n\nThe verse connects directly to the test of best in deeds. A creation found perfect invites trust that the command descending between heavens and earth is also perfect, whether in divorce limits, charity before consultation, or Friday remembrance. That trust replaces the wish to extinguish light with mouths.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think repeated looking is faithless doubt. The Quran invites it as worship, as sincere seekers look again at heavens and at the Reminder that would humble a mountain, until the same sight that sought flaws finds instead the perfection of the Most Capable who holds all authority and who guides hearts rightly through adversity.\n\nFor practice, take the invitation literally. Look at the heavens by night and day, look at your own shaped form perfected in the womb, look at provision from sources never imagined, and let each returned, weary, humbled sight increase gratitude, soften the heart before it hardens, and strengthen resolve to walk upright on the Straight Path rather than crawl facedown, trusting the Flawless Creator to also flawlessly gather for the Day of mutual loss and gain.",
  "5": "\"And indeed, We adorned the lowest heaven with ˹stars like˺ lamps, and made them ˹as missiles˺ for stoning ˹eavesdropping˺ devils, for whom We have also prepared the torment of the Blaze.\" explains a function of the flawless seven heavens just declared free of imperfection. The adornment is purposeful, not merely decorative.\n\n“And indeed, We adorned the lowest heaven with stars like lamps” describes the nearest heaven beautified with stars, those same stars whose positions form a great oath and whose lamps delight and guide. Adornment shows that the Most Compassionate who smoothed earth for you to move and created death and life to test also beautifies what is above, inviting you to look again and find no flaw. Lamps provide light for navigation, as with the camel and caravan that departed after fanfare, and for timing, as with the merging of night into day that marks prayer.\n\n“and made them as missiles for stoning eavesdropping devils” reveals a protective purpose. Devils attempt to eavesdrop on heavenly command descending between heavens, seeking to steal fragments of the unseen to distort, just as false oaths shield and secret talks grieve. Stars as missiles, or “rujuman,” repel them, preserving the well-preserved Record that no purified angel alone touches and ensuring the Reminder recited by the messenger remains uncorrupted. This shows that flawless creation includes defense of revelation, not only beauty.\n\n“for whom We have also prepared the torment of the Blaze” adds that devils’ persistent eavesdropping leads to prepared punishment, parallel to severe punishments prepared for oath-shield hypocrites, rebel societies, and residents of the Fire. Preparation signals certainty, as with Gardens prepared for those as vast as heavens and earth.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat this as astrology or as permission to fear stars. Stars are created lamps and guards, not deities, and their stoning function is unseen, not to be sought through rituals that claim to read fate. The Quran elsewhere forbids such claims, distinguishing adornment and guarding from worship.\n\nFor reflection, seeing stars as both lamps and guards encourages two attitudes. Delight in adornment with gratitude for the Best Provider who will give you flowing water if the earth’s water sinks, and trust in guarding that Revelation will be preserved despite mouths that wish to extinguish light. That trust steadies a heart tested by wealth and children, helping it remain mindful to best ability and saved from selfishness.",
  "6": "\"Those who disbelieve in their Lord will suffer the punishment of Hell. What an evil destination!\" states the recompense for those who look at flawless heavens adorned with lamps and still deny, just as those before denied despite clear proofs and tasted total loss.\n\n“Those who disbelieve in their Lord” includes those who reject the Most Compassionate who created seven heavens without flaw, who holds birds aloft, who provides flowing water, and who will not be seen to fail when heavens quake or stones are unleashed. Disbelief here is not lack of data, since layers without flaw and lamps as missiles were visible, but refusal to humble hearts at remembrance and to follow the balance sent for justice.\n\n“will suffer the punishment of Hell. What an evil destination!” echoes earlier destinations called evil for those who hindered with false shields, who swore to lies, and who fought and expelled. Hell, or “Jahannam,” with its Blaze, roaring, and fury almost bursting, is prepared for eavesdropping devils and for persistent human rebels who join their party, bound to lose. Destination, or “masir,” is final arrival after a path of saying without doing, boasting honour would expel the inferior, and forgetting Allah so He makes them forget themselves.\n\nThis follows the promise that those in awe without seeing will have forgiveness and mighty reward, showing two ways after seeing signs. One is awe that leads to guarding private talk for goodness and to lending the good loan; the other is denial that leads to Hell.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this verse condemns sincere questioners who look again and again and find themselves weary without immediate certainty. The Quran praises those who look repeatedly and are humbled, distinguishing them from those who declare “Allah has revealed nothing. You are extremely astray” after a warner did come, as the next verse notes.\n\nFor daily protection, the verse invites aligning looking with awe. Let the flawless, lamp-adorned lowest heaven remind that the One who created death and life to test which is best in deeds is also the Almighty who can punish and forgive, so the best deed today is to be among those who fear Him unseen, give thanks for hearing, sight, and intellect, and walk upright rather than persist in denial that ends evilly.",
  "7": "\"When they are tossed into it, they will hear its roaring as it boils over,\" describes the moment residents of Hell encounter the Blaze prepared for devils and for those who disbelieved in their Lord. The verse moves from destination to sensory arrival.\n\n“When they are tossed into it” translates “ulqu fiha,” cast, hurled, as stocks are thrown, indicating force and lack of honour, opposite to being admitted into Gardens under which rivers flow with light shining ahead and to the right. Tossing matches earlier expulsion without honour and horror cast into hearts of fortified peoples; now the horror is self-experienced.\n\n“they will hear its roaring as it boils over” gives sound before sight fully registers. Roaring, or “shahiq,” is a harsh, inhaling bellow, and boiling over, or “tafuru,” is surging like a pot overflowing in fury. Almost bursting in fury, as the next verse says, shows the Fire itself reacts, as if indignant that warnings were called ancient fables whenever revelations were recited and that ancient fables accusation shielded luxury. The auditory image follows the earlier silent, flawed looking that found no flaw; now the denier who refused to listen to the warner hears a roar that cannot be ignored, fulfilling “if only we had listened and reasoned.”\n\nThis hearing contrasts with the righteous who are protected from hearing the least sound of Hell and who instead hear greeting of peace, and with believers who will hear its roaring from distance as warning while still in the world through recitation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat roaring as mere metaphor for regret. The Quran presents the Blaze as real, with keepers who ask “Did a warner not come?” and with fury that is more than conscience. The purpose of vivid sound is mercy before arrival, as with stones threatened from heaven and earth swallowing, so listeners may feel secure only in sincere repentance, not in denial.\n\nFor practice, hearing this roar now through recitation should move a heart that might otherwise be diverted by wealth or children. Hear the Warner, reason about the flawless, lamp-adorned heavens, protect family from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, and choose the path of those in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, before tossing becomes the only remaining hearing.",
  "8": "\"almost bursting in fury. Every time a group is cast into it, its keepers will ask them, “Did a warner not come to you?”\" continues the scene of those tossed into the Blaze who heard its roaring as it boiled over. The fury described is not incidental heat, but intense anger at persistent denial.\n\n“almost bursting in fury” describes the Fire as if it itself is indignant, nearly splitting from rage. This personification shows that creation, from heavens adorned with lamps to the Fire prepared for devils, reacts to Allah’s command. Its fury mirrors the despicable nature of saying without doing and of repaying provision with denial; the Fire’s near-bursting reflects how abhorrent persistent covering of truth is to the One who created death and life to test best deeds.\n\n“Every time a group is cast into it, its keepers will ask them, ‘Did a warner not come to you?’” reveals procedure. Groups, or “fawj,” arrive successively, not alone, echoing earlier “you think they are united, yet hearts are divided” and the Day of Gathering where all are collected. Keepers, or “khazanah,” formidable severe angels who never disobey, question with fairness. They do not gloat; they establish that guidance was offered, removing excuse that no warner came. Warner includes Noah, Hud, Salih, Moses, Jesus who gave good news of Ahmad, and Muhammad who recites, purifies, and teaches Book and wisdom, as well as every warner sent with clear proofs whose message would humble a mountain.\n\nThe question expects the answer that follows. It also fulfills that the Messenger’s duty is only to deliver clearly; the keepers confirm delivery occurred. For the denier who thought every cry was against them like worthless planks, this cry of keepers is indeed against them, but justly.\n\nFor the living, the question is mercy while still alive. Did a warner not come to you through the Reminder, through the messenger reciting, through the flawless heavens and smoothed earth you move upon? Hearing it now allows the better reply of listening and reasoning before being asked as a group cast in.",
  "9": "\"They will reply, “Yes, a warner did come to us, but we denied and said, ‘Allah has revealed nothing. You are extremely astray.’”\" records the answer of those cast into the nearly bursting Fire when keepers ask if a warner came. The reply is honest at last, but too late to be saving.\n\n“Yes, a warner did come to us” is admission. Denying communities before, like those who tasted total loss after rebellion, and Meccan pagans who heard “Have the stories of those before not reached you?” can no longer claim ignorance. The warner came with clear proofs, with the Light, with balance so people may administer justice, and with the invitation to be humbled at remembrance before hearts hardened.\n\n“but we denied and said, ‘Allah has revealed nothing. You are extremely astray.’” shows two denials. First, denying the warner’s message itself, treating the Reminder that would humble a mountain as nothing revealed, echoing those who said when revelations were recited, “Ancient fables!” and “This is pure magic” when the foretold prophet came. Second, reversing accusation, calling the warner extremely astray while they themselves crawled facedown off the Straight Path. The same reversal appears where devoted warners were called mad and where those who wished to extinguish light with mouths called guides misguided.\n\nThe reply is sincere now because the Blaze’s roaring and the keepers’ question leave no room for false shields or impressive speech. Planks that leaned impressively now confess. That sincerity mirrors the later confession “we have been wrongdoers” after garden ruin, but timing matters. Confessing after being tossed differs from repenting before the soul reaches the throat and before the appointed time that never delays.\n\nFor the living, the usefulness is to borrow this honesty now. Admit a warner did come, that the Quran is noble in a well-preserved Record recited by a noble Messenger, that heavens without flaw and birds held aloft point to the Most Compassionate, and that wealth and children are a test. To say “Yes, a warner came” today, with listening and reasoning, prevents having to say it tomorrow when listening can no longer save from being among residents of the Blaze.",
  "10": "\"And they will lament, “If only we had listened and reasoned, we would not be among the residents of the Blaze!”\" voices the regret of those who admitted a warner came but denied. The lament identifies what was missing.\n\n“If only we had listened and reasoned” isolates two faculties. Listening, or “nasma‘,” is not mere hearing of fanfare along with caravan that drew crowds from the pulpit, but attentive hearing of the Reminder recited to bring from darkness to light. Reasoning, or “na‘qil,” is not clever boasting or divided-heart delusion, but sound understanding that connects hearing to action, like making room when told and lending the good loan. Together they form the path mocked by those who said warner is extremely astray. The deniers heard impressive speech from hypocrites and reasoned for merchandise, but did not listen to the warner or reason about flawless heavens.\n\n“we would not be among the residents of the Blaze!” recognizes that residence is earned, not arbitrary. Residents of Blaze dwell where severe punishment is prepared, where roaring is heard and fury almost bursts, while residents of Paradise alone are truly successful with Gardens under rivers. The lament shows they now see the inequality of the two residences that earlier seemed equal when they said “Should we be treated like the wicked?” and expected whatever they choose.\n\nThis echoes the garden owners who, after seeing devastation, said “If only we had said Allah willing, we would not have been deprived,” and the cry “My Lord, delay me short while to give charity and be righteous” before delay was denied. All share late clarity.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat listening and reasoning as academic virtues only. The Quran ties them to humble, practical response: listening to the call on Friday and proceeding diligently, reasoning that wealth is entrusted to be shared so it does not circulate only among the rich, and that honour belongs to Allah.\n\nFor today, the remedy is present. Listen to the Friday reminder with heart humbled, reason about the smoothed earth you walk and the birds you see held, protect family from Fire, and turn in sincere repentance so light shines ahead. Practicing listening and reasoning now transforms future lament into present light perfected and forgiven.",
  "11": "\"And so they will confess their sins. So away with the residents of the Blaze!\" closes the admission sequence after lamenting if only we had listened and reasoned. Confession leads not to pardon in that place, but to confirmation of justice.\n\n“And so they will confess their sins” translates “fa-‘tarafu bi-dhanbihim,” they acknowledge wrongs, not as vague mistake, but as sins against clear command, like rebelling against commandments of Lord and His messengers and tasting severe account. Sins include disbelief in their Lord despite flawless heavens, saying Allah revealed nothing, calling the warner astray, and persisting in arrogance and aversion when provided for if He withholds. Confession here mirrors Pharaoh’s wife’s household examples where betrayal was named, and the garden owners confessing “we have been transgressors.” In the world, confession with sincere repentance can bring absolution, Gardens, and light perfected; after being tossed, confession confirms the record kept while they forgot and the informing on the Day.\n\n“So away with the residents of the Blaze!” is a declaration of distance, or “suhqan,” meaning removal, banishment, away with them. It is not a curse from people but a divine pronouncement that they are far from mercy, as the wall with gate placed grace on one side and torment on the other, and as the Fire became the only fitting home for oath-shield hinderers. Away indicates they are driven from nearness that the Most Compassionate offers to those in awe without seeing Him, whose forgiveness and mighty reward follow.\n\nThis follows the pattern that Satan’s party is bound to lose and Allah and His messengers will prevail, and that residents of Fire cannot be equal to residents of Paradise. Distance emphasizes inequality.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think confession always saves regardless of timing. The Quran shows timing matters because sincere repentance before the soul reaches the throat and before gathering is accepted, while confession after tossing is mere acknowledgment of truth already made clear by the warner, similar to fasting after the appointed time never delays.\n\nFor the living, confess now. Say with the garden owners, “Glory be to our Lord, we have been wrongdoers,” and with later supplicants, “Forgive us and our brothers,” and with sincere repentance that asks perfected light, so confession leads to distance from Blaze rather than confirmation of it.",
  "12": "\"Indeed, those in awe of their Lord without seeing Him will have forgiveness and a mighty reward.\" shifts from those who confessed too late to those whose awe before seeing prevents that fate. The verse defines the successful listeners and reasoners.\n\n“Indeed, those in awe of their Lord without seeing Him” translates “yakhshawna rabbahum bil-ghayb,” fearing, revering, and being humbled unseen, when alone, in secret talk, or in market after Friday dispersal. Awe without seeing is the core test, as He created death and life to test best deeds, as birds are held unseen by the Most Compassionate, and as the Knower of unseen and seen knows whatever you conceal. It contrasts with those who needed roaring to hear and who said a warner is astray until the Blaze made denial impossible. Awe without seeing led the wife of Pharaoh to pray for a house near Allah despite palace power, and Mary to guard chastity and testify devoutly.\n\n“will have forgiveness and a mighty reward” promises two gains. Forgiveness, or “maghfirah,” covers sins that even the best in deeds accumulate, as every soul is told to look to what it sent for tomorrow and fear Allah, who is All-Aware. Mighty reward, or “ajr kabir,” is the great, ultimate triumph of Gardens under rivers for ever and ever, splendid homes, and excellent provision, the same mighty reward for those who lend good loans and are saved from selfishness.\n\nThis follows the fate of residents of Blaze so that awe is seen as prevention, not parable. Those in awe listened when the warner said “Allah has revealed a Reminder and sent a messenger reciting,” and they reasoned that seven heavens without flaw indicate the Most Compassionate’s flawless command.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit awe to trembling without action. Awe without seeing produces hearing, obeying, spending in charity, protecting families, and guarding trusts, as detailed in divorce limits and Friday commands.\n\nFor practice, cultivate unseen awe. Remember Allah whether speaking secretly or openly, knowing He knows what is hidden in the heart; be Subtle, All-Aware aware that He could not know His own creation? That unseen awe, expressed in mindful charity and sincere repentance that perfects light, secures forgiveness that covers and mighty reward that remains when the Blaze’s residents are told away.",
  "13": "\"Whether you speak secretly or openly—He surely knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.\" grounds awe without seeing in perfect knowledge. The verse follows forgiveness and mighty reward for the unseen-aweful, now explaining why secret or open makes no difference to the Knower.\n\n“Whether you speak secretly or openly” covers the two modes of human speech. Secret speech was earlier exposed as private talks that grieve believers when inspired by Satan, as whispering to keep poor from garden, and as false oaths as shields. Open speech includes boasting honour will expel inferior, saying “ancient fables” to revelations, and professing “we bear witness” loudly. Both are speech acts the All-Hearing hears, whether the woman pleaded with the Prophet and Allah heard the exchange from above heavens or the hypocrites said “do not spend on those with the Messenger” to one another. Secret and open are equal before the One who is with you wherever you are.\n\n“He surely knows best what is hidden in the heart” states deeper knowing. Hearts hide bitterness toward believers that later supplicants ask not to allow, love for emigrants that prefers them despite need, stinginess that withholds good, and awe that listens and reasons. “Knows best” indicates His knowledge surpasses self-knowledge and cannot be deceived by impressive appearance that impresses or planks leaned worthless. This echoes that Allah knows whatever is in heavens and earth, whatever you conceal and reveal, and that He knows best what is hidden.\n\nThis prepares for the rhetorical challenge “How could He not know His Own creation? For He alone is Most Subtle, All-Aware.” If He created hearing, sight, and intellect yet you hardly thank, and smoothed earth for you to eat, then not knowing hearts would contradict creation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use knowledge as excuse for passivity, as if nothing can be hidden so effort is pointless. The Quran uses knowledge to invite correction before informing on the Day.\n\nFor daily conduct, this verse encourages aligning secret and open. Speak goodness and righteousness in private, leave business for remembrance openly on Friday, pardon family faults secret resentment would harbor, and give hidden charity that the Most Appreciative multiplies, trusting that open and secret are both best known by the One whose mighty reward awaits the unseen-aweful.",
  "14": "\"How could He not know His Own creation? For He ˹alone˺ is the Most Subtle, All-Aware.\" answers the previous verse with a logical challenge that reinforces awe without seeing and prepares for the smoothed earth and warnings of swallowing.\n\n“How could He not know His Own creation?” is a rhetorical question expecting “He certainly knows.” If He created death and life to test, shaped you perfecting form, brought you into being and gave hearing, sight, intellect, dispersed you over earth, and adorned the lowest heaven with lamps, then not knowing the created would be impossible. The question uses creation as proof of knowledge, similar to how creation from a drop proved resurrection easy. As the Maker of the flawless seven heavens knows every fissure that does not exist, so He knows every whisper in a heart.\n\n“For He alone is the Most Subtle, All-Aware” gives two names. Most Subtle, or “al-Latif,” reaches the finest details, hidden motives like boycotting emigrants to break them away, boasting honour, or quietly preferring others despite need, as well as the smallest bird’s wing movement held aloft. All-Aware, or “al-Khabir,” is thoroughly informed, encompassing the secret talk of three where He is fourth and the open declaration before keepers that a warner did come. Together they describe penetration and comprehensiveness, subtle reach and fully informed grasp, as with the command descending between seven heavens.\n\nThis directly counters the delusion that secret speech escapes, that impressive plank-like speech impresses the Knower, or that wealth and children divert unnoticed. It also comforts the unseen-aweful, whose hidden awe and mighty reward are seen subtly and known awarely, even without public fanfare.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think subtlety implies leniency toward hidden wrongs. Subtle knowledge ensures precise justice, as with severe account for rebellious societies and horrible punishment tasted.\n\nFor practice, remember the Subtle, All-Aware in both creation and conduct. Notice subtle provisions like water that could sink and be brought as flowing water again, give subtle charity that only He sees, and correct subtle resentments in the heart, trusting that the One who knows His creation will guide hearts through adversity for those who have faith, and will inform each soul of what it did on the Day He gathers for mutual loss and gain.",
  "15": "\"He is the One Who smoothed out the earth for you, so move about in its regions and eat from His provisions. And to Him is the resurrection ˹of all˺.\" links creation knowledge to earthly livelihood and final return. After establishing that the Most Subtle, All-Aware must know creation, the verse shows one tangible creation, the smoothed earth.\n\n“He is the One Who smoothed out the earth for you” translates “dhallala lakum al-ard,” made it pliable, subdued, and manageable, not jagged impassable barriers, so foot can walk and plow can cut. Smoothing echoes the seven heavens layered without flaw and the adorned lamps; it extends flawless making to the ground you touch, after heavens you see. The smoothing is for you, indicating purposeful provision, like shade extended, water flowing, fruit abundant, and earth as test of best deeds.\n\n“so move about in its regions and eat from His provisions” gives permission and task. Move, or “famshu fi manakibiha,” walk in its shoulders, its pathways, highlands and lowlands, dispatching after Friday prayer to seek bounty and remember often. Eat from His provisions, or “kulu min rizqihi,” enjoy lawful provision, including harvests that delight planters then become chaff, and water that could be withheld or made salty, now offered to eat with thanks. The command is not to hoard or boast, but to move lawfully and eat gratefully, lending the good loan and providing according to means for waiting women.\n\n“And to Him is the resurrection of all” anchors movement and eating in return. Resurrection here is “nushur,” rising and spreading forth, as earlier gathered for the Day of Gathering and as earlier “We will surely be resurrected then informed.” The One who smoothed earth for walking can gather those who walked, and the One who provides can ask about thanks. This prevents moving and eating from becoming diversion that makes true losers.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read “eat from His provisions” as license for waste or for taking provision that belongs to orphans, poor, or emigrants, or for refusing to spend on those with the Messenger. Smoothing is a test that expects hearing, obeying, and spending.\n\nFor daily walk, move about regions for sustenance and family care, eat with “if only we had listened and reasoned” in mind, and remember each step and bite points toward the resurrection to the Most Capable who holds all authority and who asks to perfect light, so provision becomes worship and the earth’s smoothing becomes path to Gardens rather than to the Blaze whose keepers ask about warners.",
  "16": "\"Do you feel secure that the One Who is in heaven will not cause the earth to swallow you up as it quakes violently?\" poses the first of two security tests after stating earth was smoothed for you and to Him is resurrection. The question is not about location of Allah, but about feeling secure against His warning.\n\n“Do you feel secure that the One Who is in heaven will not cause the earth to swallow you up as it quakes violently?” translates “a-amintum man fi al-sama’.” Classical commentators explain “Who is in heaven” as referring to Allah’s authority above creation, the King, the Most High and Most Near who is with you wherever you are, whose command descends between seven heavens, whose knowledge encompasses heavens and earth. The phrase invites awe of the One above, not a spatial confinement. The question tests complacency. After being given smoothed earth to walk and eat, do you assume it will never shake, despite stories of those before who denied and were then how severe was My response, and despite true losers diverted by wealth and children?\n\n“cause the earth to swallow you up as it quakes violently” recalls the quaking at the Inevitable Event when earth will be shaken violently and mountains crushed, but also local swallowing like Qarun, whose treasures could not help. Quaking, or “tamuru,” is churning, shifting, as smoothed shoulders become swallowing depths. The same Subtle, All-Aware who smoothed can also shake.\n\nThis follows the command to move and eat. Permission is not immunity. The One who permits movement also warns, as with the caravan fanfare that tempted leaving the pulpit, thereby testing whether provision leads to thanks or to delusion of security.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read “in heaven” as suggesting Allah occupies a place like creation, or to use the verse to instill paralyzing fear that prevents moving and seeking bounty. The Quran gives the warning to prevent arrogance and aversion persisted in, not to stop lawful seeking. Moving with remembrance and spending to protect families transforms security into gratitude.\n\nFor reflection, do not feel secure while persisting in saying “ancient fables” or hindering with false shields. Feel secure in Him by being among those in awe without seeing Him, who hear, obey, and spend to best ability, trusting that if earth were to quake, the return is still to Him whose forgiveness and mighty reward await the mindful.",
  "17": "\"Or do you feel secure that the One Who is in heaven will not unleash upon you a storm of stones. Only then would you know how ˹serious˺ My warning was!\" poses the second security test, adding aerial threat to the earthly swallowing. The pair covers below and above, completing the warning after smoothing.\n\n“Or do you feel secure that the One Who is in heaven will not unleash upon you a storm of stones” translates “hasiban,” a wind carrying pebbles, like that which destroyed ‘Ad with furious, bitter wind for seven nights and eight days or the companions of the elephant. While swallowing comes from beneath smoothed shoulders you walk on, stones come from above the adorned lamps you admire. Both are within the Hands that hold all authority and are Most Capable of everything, whose treasures belong solely to Allah yet hypocrites do not comprehend. The question tests whether the same people who felt secure because they saw no flaw after looking again and again assume security means immunity from warning.\n\n“Only then would you know how serious My warning was!” states the tragic timing of realization. Warning, or “nudhur,” includes warner’s coming, the Light recited, and the Reminder that would humble a mountain. Knowing seriousness after stones fall mirrors knowing after being tossed and hearing roaring, or after confessing “if only we had listened.” Then is too late for benefit, similar to crying “delay me” when the appointed time never delays. The knowledge then is not saving; it is regret that confirms the warner was extremely astray accusation was false.\n\nThis pair follows “Have they not seen the birds held” and “Which powerless force will help you instead of the Most Compassionate?” to show no helper exists if He withholds. Feeling secure in delusion is precisely “they persist in arrogance and aversion.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to live in constant dread that stones fall at any moment, abandoning moving and eating. The Quran presents warnings to instill balanced awe, not to paralyze lawful seeking after Friday dispersal. Security is found not in denying warning, but in responding: be among those in awe without seeing, whose sins are forgiven, who speak truthfully whether secretly or openly, and who trust the Most Subtle, All-Aware who also smooths earth for you, so warning remains a call to perfect light rather than a storm that teaches too late.",
  "18": "\"And certainly those before them denied ˹as well˺, then how severe was My response!\" anchors the two warnings of swallowing and stones in historical precedent. The verse moves from hypothetical security to actual history.\n\n“And certainly those before them denied as well” refers to societies before the present deniers, including Thamud destroyed by blast, ‘Ad by furious wind, Pharaoh seized with crushing grip, and the garden owners reduced to ashes after swearing to harvest without saying Allah willing. Denial before took the same forms: mocking “How can humans be guides?” and “Allah has revealed nothing,” hindering with false oaths, boasting honour will expel inferior, and taking light lightly.\n\n“then how severe was My response!” invites reflection on outcome. Severe response, or “nakir,” is denial made manifest as requital, the evil consequences tasted and total loss that followed. It echoes “We called each society to severe account and subjected them to horrible punishment” and “they tasted evil consequences, outcome was total loss.” Severity matches persistence, not a single slip. The question expects you to recall stories that have reached you pagans and to imagine how severe the retort was when the Fire nearly bursts in fury and keepers ask about warners.\n\nThis follows the smoothed earth and the two security questions, showing that feeling secure is historically refuted. Those who felt secure in strongholds that they thought put them out of reach were reached from where they never expected, with horror cast and houses destroyed by their own hands and believers’ hands. So take a lesson, O people of insight, remains the refrain.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read severity as divine harshness detached from mercy. The same Quran that describes adorned lamps and provision to eat also describes forgiveness and mighty reward for those in awe without seeing, and way out and provision from unimaginable sources for the mindful. Severity was after repeated messengers with clear proofs, not sudden.\n\nFor today, the severity remembered should not breed despair about personal lapses, but urgency to listen and reason, to protect families from Fire, and to turn in sincere repentance before the earth quakes or stones fall, before the appointed time that never delays arrives, seeking the response that is forgiveness and mighty reward rather than severe nakir.",
  "19": "\"Have they not seen the birds above them, spreading and folding their wings? None holds them up except the Most Compassionate. Indeed, He is All-Seeing of everything.\" turns from warnings of swallowing and stones and from histories of severe response to a gentle, daily sign that demonstrates the same authority with mercy.\n\n“Have they not seen the birds above them, spreading and folding their wings?” invites looking upward, continuing the command to look again and again at flawless heavens and adorned lamps. Birds are visible on the smoothed earth where you move and eat, a sign between swallowing below and stones above, showing the middle realm also governed. Spreading and folding, or “saffat wa yaqbidna,” describes the rhythmic opening and closing that enables flight, a subtle control that the viewer can observe without instruments.\n\n“None holds them up except the Most Compassionate” attributes flight to mercy, not to chance. The Most Compassionate, who created seven heavens in layers without flaw, who smoothed earth, and who created death and life to test, is the holder. The name “al-Rahman” here stresses that holding is an act of compassion, sustaining even small creatures that neither sow as you sow to be asked “is it you who cause it to grow?” nor control provision. If He holds birds through precisely tuned air, wings, and instinct, He can hold you through tests of wealth and children without letting wealth or children divert, and can hold your provision if He withholds, as the next verse asks.\n\n“Indeed, He is All-Seeing of everything” closes with sight that matches earlier knowledge of seen and unseen and Most Subtle, All-Aware. All-Seeing observes birds’ wings, the looked-at heavens, the secret talk, and the openly declared boast.\n\nThis verse corrects arrogance and aversion that persists, showing delusion that a powerless force could help instead of the Most Compassionate. No force holds birds, none brings provision if He withholds, none prevents swallowing or stones.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this denies aerodynamics, as earlier that fire’s trees being from Allah denies botany. The Quran affirms created means while attributing ultimate holding to the Most Compassionate who created the means.\n\nFor practice, see birds and remember the Holder. Move about regions with trust, eat from provision with thanks, walk upright on the Straight Path, and be among those in awe without seeing, whose forgiveness and mighty reward are held by the same compassionate Hand that holds birds, inviting you to look, reason, and be grateful for hearing, sight, and intellect you hardly thank.",
  "20": "\"Also, which ˹powerless˺ force will come to your help instead of the Most Compassionate? Indeed, the disbelievers are only ˹lost˺ in delusion.\" challenges reliance on any helper besides the Holder of birds. The question follows the bird sign after warnings of swallowing and stones, exposing delusion.\n\n“Also, which powerless force will come to your help instead of the Most Compassionate?” translates “amman hadha alladhi huwa jundun lakum yansurukum min duni al-Rahman,” what soldiers or party do you have that would help you besides the Most Compassionate? Powerless force, or “jund,” evokes troops, strongholds, allies, wealth, children, honour, and merchandise that impressed and diverted. Instead of the Most Compassionate is key. The One who holds birds, smoothed earth, adorned heavens with lamps as missiles against devils, and created death and life to test is the true helper. Any other helper, whether hypocrites who said “we will leave with you and we will help you” but never left, or fortified strongholds thought to put them out of reach, is powerless without His permission.\n\n“Indeed, the disbelievers are only lost in delusion” diagnoses the state of those who trust such forces. Delusion, or “ghurur,” is the same delusion of worldly life as play, amusement, luxury, boasting, and competition that becomes chaff, the delight of planters that withers. They think impressive planks leaning will hold, that false oaths shield, that boycotting emigrants will break them away, that honour expels inferior at will. All are delusion because they forget that treasuries belong to Allah and honour belongs to Allah.\n\nThis echoes earlier “which powerless force will help you instead of the Most Compassionate?” and prepares for “who will provide for you if He withholds provision?” The two questions dismantle the two pillars of false security: protection and provision.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this forbids seeking help from people, like calling two reliable men to witness or consulting together courteously. The Quran commands using lawful helps while knowing ultimate help is from the Most Compassionate; helpers are means, not instead.\n\nFor reflection, place help where it belongs. Seek lawful help in family, work, and community, but put trust in the Most Compassionate who is with you wherever you are and who knows what is hidden in the heart. That corrects arrogance and aversion, returns sight from frustrated weariness to grateful awe, and moves a person from delusion to the upright walk on the Straight Path that is rightly guided.",
  "21": "\"Or who is it that will provide for you if He withholds His provision? In fact, they persist in arrogance and aversion ˹to the truth˺.\" poses the second helper question, shifting from protection to provision. After asking which force will help instead of the Most Compassionate, the verse asks who will provide if He withholds.\n\n“Or who is it that will provide for you if He withholds His provision?” continues the treasury theme. Treasuries of heavens and earth belong to Allah, honour belongs to Allah, and earth is smoothed to move and eat from His provision, but provision is His to give and withhold. Provision here includes rain that could be made salty, harvest that could be chaff, water that could sink then be brought as flowing water, and family maintenance according to means. If He withholds, no merchant caravan with fanfare, no garden promising early harvest, and no boycott-proof solidarity can create rain or growth. The question exposes delusion that merchandise provides independently while remembrance is left.\n\n“In fact, they persist in arrogance and aversion to the truth” diagnoses response to this clear dependence. Persistence, or “lajju fi ‘utuw wa nufur,” is stubbornness in haughtiness and fleeing. Arrogance is the same pride that made hypocrites turn heads in disgust when told to seek forgiveness, that made impressive planks think every cry against them, and that makes people say “ancient fables” when revelations are recited. Aversion is fleeing, as from the warner they called extremely astray, as from the Reminder that would humble a mountain, as from the crosser who crawls facedown rather than walk upright.\n\nThis follows the bird sign and the powerless force question, showing that delusion has two supports, false help and false provision, both from the Most Compassionate.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think withholding provision means immediate starvation for the arrogant, contradicting observed delay. Withholding can be gradual, a way out not made, hearts not made easy, communities tasting loss after rebellion, and the ultimate withholding on the Day of mutual loss.\n\nFor practice, the provision question invites turning persistence from arrogance to awe. Ask instead who will provide if He withholds, then be grateful for hearing, sight, intellect, and for dispersed gathering, eat from provision with thanks, lend the good loan, and protect families through mindful spending, so persistence shifts from aversion to the Straight Path the upright walker follows, under the Best Provider who loves those who are fair and who multiplies what is given.",
  "22": "\"Who is ˹rightly˺ guided: the one who crawls facedown or the one who walks upright on the Straight Path?\" offers a vivid comparison after exposing delusion of trusting powerless forces and delusion of provision without the Provider. The contrast is between two walkers on the smoothed earth just described.\n\n“The one who crawls facedown” pictures a person fallen on face, blind, stumbling, unable to see the flawless heavens, the birds held, or the adorned lamps, and persisting in arrogance and aversion. Crawling facedown is the posture of the deluded who rejected the warner as extremely astray, who claimed Allah revealed nothing, and who are lost in delusion thinking merchandise and fanfare are better than remembrance. It recalls the Inevitable Event when earth is shaken and the sky torn, but now as moral posture before that Day, where faces become gloomy when torment draws near.\n\n“The one who walks upright on the Straight Path” is the counterpart, the upright walker who looks again and again without finding flaw, who is in awe without seeing, who listens and reasons, who moves about regions and eats from provision with thanks, who hears and obeys and spends to best ability. Upright walk is the Straight Path, or “sirat mustaqim,” the balance and Light that bring from darkness to light, that include making room in gatherings, guarding chastity, being true to trusts, and protecting families.\n\nThe question expects the upright walker is rightly guided, yet it is asked rather than stated to invite reasoning, as with “how could He not know His creation?” Reasoning here is the same reasoning that the deniers lacked and later lamented, “if only we had reasoned.”\n\nThis verse links to the earlier test of best in deeds. Best is the upright walk, which the Almighty, All-Forgiving rewards with mighty Forgiveness and Gardens.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read crawling as disability. The Quran uses crawling as moral metaphor, not mockery of physical impairment; it judges obstinate refusal to see flawless signs, not bodies. Upright walk is available to every soul that turns in sincere repentance, whose light will shine ahead.\n\nFor daily choice, the question is practical. Choose the walk that lifts the face toward the flawless heavens and the smoothed earth’s provision, that protects family, that answers Friday call diligently and leaves business, that perfects light, rather than the crawl that flees the warner and persists in delusion until provision is withheld and no helper appears.",
  "23": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “He is the One Who brought you into being and gave you hearing, sight, and intellect. ˹Yet˺ you hardly give any thanks.”\" grounds the choice between crawling and walking upright in gratitude for faculties. The verse is a direct reminder after warnings of swallowing, stones, severe response, and delusion.\n\n“He is the One Who brought you into being” translates “ansha’akum,” originated, raised up, as He created you, shaped you perfecting form, dispersed you over earth, and smoothed it for you. Bringing into being includes the drop, the infant, and the community raised among the unlettered with a messenger reciting, purifying, and teaching.\n\n“and gave you hearing, sight, and intellect” lists three gifts that enable listening and reasoning, the very pair the deniers later wish they had used. Hearing receives the warner, the keepers’ question, and Friday remembrance; sight looks again at flawless heavens, adorned lamps, and birds held; intellect, or “af’idah,” hearts and understanding, processes what is heard and seen into awe without seeing. Together they are tools to test which is best in deeds and to avoid being diverted by wealth and children.\n\n“Yet you hardly give any thanks” observes ingratitude, “qalilan ma tashkurun,” little thanks is given. Hardly thanking manifests as saying “ancient fables” when revelations are recited, calling clear proofs magic, marking the Prophet’s snout in mockery, and persisting in arrogance and aversion despite provision that could be withheld. Hardly thanking also appears as neglecting to hear, obey, and spend to best ability, and as forgetting Allah so He makes them forget themselves.\n\nThis follows the upright walk question, showing which walk thanks. Upright thanks through using hearing to listen, sight to look again, and intellect to reason, leading to protecting families, being true to trusts, and lending good loans.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think thanks is merely words. Thanks is using faculties for their purpose, as with moving about earth and eating provision with remembrance often so you may be successful.\n\nFor practice, thank specifically. Use hearing to listen diligently when call is made on Friday, use sight to see birds and lamps as signs, use intellect to connect smoothed earth to resurrection to Him, and translate thanks into action, being mindful, hearing, obeying, spending, and walking upright, trusting the Most Capable who gave these gifts to also forgive and grant mighty reward to the grateful.",
  "24": "\"˹Also˺ say, “He is the One Who has dispersed you ˹all˺ over the earth, and to Him you will ˹all˺ be gathered.”\" adds dispersion and gathering to the creation thanks. After “He brought you into being and gave hearing, sight, intellect,” the verse maps spatial history from origin to end.\n\n“He is the One Who has dispersed you all over the earth” translates “dhara’akum fi al-ard,” scattered and multiplied across regions of the smoothed earth you were told to move about and eat from. Dispersion is the current state, as with the garden owners’ field, the caravan’s travel, and the hyocrites’ boast “if we return to Medina” about expelling. Dispersion includes earlier and later generations, illiterate peoples and those not yet joined, all spread as test.\n\n“and to Him you will all be gathered” translates “wa ilayhi tuhsharun,” you will be collected, as for the Day He will gather you all for the Day of Gathering, the mutual loss and gain, when He informs you of what you have done and that is easy for Allah, when the Inevitable Event crushes earth and mountains with one blow and eight angels bear the Throne. Gathering reverses dispersion. The same One who dispersed with wisdom can collect with ease, just as He holds birds aloft and could withhold provision.\n\nThis follows hardly thanking, showing the gathered will be thankers and ingrates sorted, not equal as asked “Should We treat the submitted like the wicked? How do you judge?” Gathering is where that judgment is answered.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear dispersion as exile without return, as the expelled thought their strongholds out of reach or as the garden owners thought deprivation was loss of way. Dispersion is purposeful test, and gathering is purposeful accounting.\n\nFor daily life, remember dispersion is for moving and eating with remembrance often to be successful, and gathering is for resurrection of all. Walk upright, hear and obey, spend, and protect families, so gathering finds you among those in awe without seeing, whose light will shine ahead and to the right and who will say “Perfect our light and forgive us,” rather than among those who will lament if only we had listened and reasoned and who will hear the Fire nearly bursting.",
  "25": "\"˹Still˺ they ask ˹the believers˺, “When will this threat come to pass, if what you say is true?”\" reports the sceptics’ challenge after dispersion and gathering were asserted. The threat is the gathering, the torment that draws near with gloomy faces, the swallowing, the stones, the Fire that will be asked “Did a warner not come?”\n\n“They ask the believers, ‘When will this threat come to pass, if what you say is true?’” mimics earlier “When will this threat come?” used to mock the Inevitable Hour and the appointed time that never delays. The tone is not sincere inquiry like “which powerless force will help?” but dismissal, similar to “How can humans be our guides?” and “Ancient fables!” The question treats the Most Compassionate’s warning as a timetable to be debated rather than a truth to prepare for, as with the caravan fanfare that drew crowds from the pulpit to ask immediate profit rather than eternal provision.\n\nThe condition “if what you say is true” pretends to test truthfulness, but actually reveals delusion that true warning must be immediate, as with those who said “Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?” and thought delay meant falsehood. It ignores that the warner’s duty is only to deliver clearly and that the One who is in heaven gives respite to test best deeds before stones.\n\nThis question follows hardly thanking and dispersion to show ingratitude’s effect. Hearts that hardly thank for hearing, sight, intellect still demand a date rather than listen and reason.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to answer with speculation, setting dates as the threatened Day fifty thousand years or the Trumpet with one blast, beyond knowledge. The next verse instructs the Prophet to say knowledge is with Allah alone and he is only a clear warner, preserving the unseen as seen and unseen known best by the Knower.\n\nFor believers, the proper response to “When?” is not argument about dates, but invitation to prepare. Move about earth, eat provision thankfully, be mindful to best ability, and turn in sincere repentance so that when the threat does come, whether soon with a quake or later with gathering, you are among those whose faces are not gloomy but whose light is being perfected, whose sins are absolved, and whose Gardens under rivers for ever and ever await the ultimate triumph.",
  "26": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “That knowledge is with Allah alone, and I am only sent with a clear warning.”\" answers the “When will this threat come?” challenge with a boundary and a role. The verse follows the sceptics’ timing demand after dispersion and gathering were taught.\n\n“That knowledge is with Allah alone” translates “innama al-‘ilmu ‘inda Allah,” the knowledge of when the threat, the gathering, the swallowing, the stones, the Inevitable Event, and the Trumpet will occur belongs to Allah, the Knower of seen and unseen, the Most Subtle, All-Aware whose command descends between seven heavens and who created death and life to test. It echoes “That knowledge is with Allah alone” after birds and provision, and “So be patient with your Lord’s decree and do not be like the Man of the Whale,” showing that even messengers are given “perhaps” and “soon you will know” rather than calendars. The appointed time that never delays is known precisely to the All-Knowing, All-Wise who has set a destiny for everything, but its date is not disclosed to hasten hearts through trust rather than calculation.\n\n“and I am only sent with a clear warning” defines the Prophet’s task. “Only” limits, as with “Our Messenger’s duty is only to deliver clearly.” Clear warning, or “nadhir mubin,” is the warner role whose keepers later ask “Did a warner not come?” and who recites, purifies, and teaches. Warning is clear because proof is clear: flawless heavens you are told to look again at, adorned lamps, birds held, earth smoothed, hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked, and histories that tasted total loss. Warning does not need a date to be clear; its clarity is in the call to listen, reason, and walk upright.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think “knowledge with Allah alone” means warning is vague. Warning is vivid: Fire nearly bursting, faces gloomy, Fire as evil destination, and Gardens as ultimate triumph, all made clear so you may take a lesson, O people of insight.\n\nFor practice, this verse teaches humility in the face of the unseen. Do not claim dates, do not demand them, do not be deluded that delay means deliverance. Instead, be among those in awe without seeing, who hear and obey and spend to best ability, dispersing to seek bounty and remembering often, so that when knowledge becomes sight and torment draws near, you hear “This is what you claimed would never come” as a confirmation for others, while you are told “away with residents of Blaze” is not for you but forgiveness and mighty reward is.",
  "27": "\"Then when they see the torment drawing near, the faces of the disbelievers will become gloomy, and it will be said ˹to them˺, “This is what you claimed would never come.”\" describes the visible confirmation of the warning whose timing sceptics demanded. The verse moves from “knowledge with Allah alone and I am only a clear warner” to the moment knowledge becomes sight.\n\n“Then when they see the torment drawing near, the faces of the disbelievers will become gloomy” translates “si’at wujuh,” faces become evil, darkened, disfigured by distress. Gloom is the opposite of light shining ahead and to the right for believers whose light is perfected and who are not disgraced with the Prophet. Faces tell hearts, as impressive appearance that once impressed now collapses; worthless planks leaned show inner hollowness outwardly. Drawing near indicates torment approached while they felt secure that earth would not swallow and stones would not fall, persisting in arrogance and aversion.\n\n“and it will be said to them, ‘This is what you claimed would never come’” or “idd‘a‘ta tad‘un,” what you used to call for or press for in mockery, demanding “When will this threat come if you are truthful?” The same mouths that said “You are extremely astray” to warners and “Ancient fables!” to recited revelations now hear their own demand returned as fulfillment. The announcement is not new teaching but exposure of earlier denial, as keepers asked “Did a warner not come?” and they replied “Yes, but we denied.”\n\nThis mirrors the Day of Gathering’s mutual loss and gain where loss becomes visible, and the Fire’s keepers’ questioning. It also fulfills that Allah’s warning seriousness would be known only then.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine gloomy faces as mere sadness. Gloom here is humiliation plus despair that ransom by children, spouses, siblings, clan, even everyone on earth cannot secure, as later described for the wicked.\n\nFor the living, the cure is present. The threat is still not yet seen, so faces need not become gloomy. Listen now, reason now, protect families from Fire, walk upright, and turn in sincere repentance so that when torment draws near for others, your face is among those whose light is being perfected and whose greeting is “Peace,” and you are told to enter Paradise, not reminded what you once claimed would never come.",
  "28": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Consider this: whether Allah causes me and those with me to die or shows us mercy, who will save the disbelievers from a painful punishment?”\" asks the deniers to consider the irrelevance of their wish against the Prophet. The question follows gloomy faces when torment draws near and the claim “This is what you used to claim would never come.”\n\n“Consider this: whether Allah causes me and those with me to die or shows us mercy” presents two possibilities for the Prophet and believers. Death may come as appointment never delayed, or mercy may be shown through help, victory, Garden, and perfected light. Both are in the Hands that hold all authority and are Most Capable. The deniers had hoped that the Prophet’s death would end the warning, similar to hypocrites who awaited demise of believers and thought every cry was against them. The verse counters that the Prophet’s fate does not determine theirs. Whether he lives or is returned to the Knower of seen and unseen, their accountability remains.\n\n“who will save the disbelievers from a painful punishment?” is the thrust. No powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate can save them, as just asked, and no provision can be provided if He withholds. The question exposes that their fixation on the Messenger’s mortality diverts from their own exposure to the Blaze that almost bursts and whose keepers ask about warners. It also echoes that neither wealth nor children will help against Allah and that oaths as shields will not help.\n\nThis mirrors earlier “Consider if your water were to sink, who could bring flowing water?” and “He brought you into being with hearing, sight, intellect” yet you hardly thank. All three “consider” calls invite reasoning rather than mocking.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as curse that the Prophet wishes their punishment. It is a logical challenge to shift focus from gloating over a man’s death to preparing for the inevitable gathering where mutual loss and gain become clear.\n\nFor practice, the verse steadies believers facing hostility. Your mission is to deliver clearly, to be among those in awe without seeing, and to trust the Most Compassionate in whom you believe and trust; the deniers’ fate does not hinge on your lifespan but on their own listening and reasoning before torment draws near.",
  "29": "\"Say, “He is the Most Compassionate—in Him ˹alone˺ we believe, and in Him ˹alone˺ we trust. You will soon know who is clearly astray.”\" declares the believer’s anchor after asking who will save disbelievers if the Prophet dies or is shown mercy. The verse defines faith and trust.\n\n“He is the Most Compassionate—in Him alone we believe” identifies the One whose creation shows no flaw, who holds birds, smooths earth, adorns lamps, and creates death and life to test best deeds. Belief in the Most Compassionate includes belief that He is King, Holy, Almighty, All-Wise whose Hands hold all authority and whose command descends between seven heavens. It includes that whatever is in heavens and earth constantly glorifies Him, that He knows whatever you conceal and reveal, and that He is Most Subtle, All-Aware. Believing alone in Him contrasts with those who claimed delight in merchandise better than remembrance and who thought honour expels inferior by tribal power.\n\n“and in Him alone we trust” translates “wa ‘alayhi tawakkalna,” placing reliance exclusively, as commanded “So in Allah let the believers put their trust” after “there is no god except Him.” Trust here includes putting trust after hearing and obeying and spending to best ability, after moving about earth and eating provision knowing to Him is resurrection, and after seeking forgiveness and mighty reward as those in awe without seeing. Trust corrects both arrogance that persists and aversion that flees.\n\n“You will soon know who is clearly astray” promises clarification. Soon, or “sa-ta‘lamun,” indicates near future when torment draws near with gloomy faces, when the Inevitable Hour and the Striking Disaster strike, when the Trumpet is blown and earth and mountains crushed, when mutual loss and gain is displayed. The clearly astray will be distinguished from the upright walker on the Straight Path, not by impressive speech or wealth and children, but by who was in awe unseen.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as threat of personal vindication. It is the prophetic warning that truth will become visible, as keepers ask about warners and confession proves warner did come.\n\nFor daily life, repeating “In Him we believe, in Him we trust” re-centers after being diverted by wealth or children, after hearing fanfare, after facing boycott. It turns the heart from debating “When will this threat come?” to preparing with hearing, sight, intellect thanks and sincere repentance that perfects light.",
  "30": "\"Say, “Consider this: if your water were to sink ˹into the earth˺, then who ˹else˺ could bring you flowing water?”\" closes Surah al-Mulk with the most tangible dependence. After declaring belief and trust in the Most Compassionate, the verse asks about water, the provision that makes smoothed earth livable.\n\n“Consider this: if your water were to sink into the earth” envisions the wells, springs, and rivers that provide drinking, irrigation, and wudu drying up, sinking deep beyond reach, as earth could swallow as it quakes violently if security is presumed. Sinking mirrors the swallowing threatened earlier and the Boats carried with Noah when floodwater overflowed, showing water’s dual role as mercy and warning. TheMost Compassionate who smoothed earth and adorned lamps can also cause water to sink, as He can make sweet water salty if He willed, and reduce harvest to chaff leaving you to lament “we have been deprived.”\n\n“then who else could bring you flowing water?” translates “ma‘in,” running, gushing water that flows as with Gardens under rivers, the same flowing water promised as provision never out of season nor forbidden. The question expects “no one except Allah,” the Best Provider, the Lord of treasuries, the Most Capable of everything whose Hands hold authority. No powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate can bring it, no merchandise can purchase it if withheld, no stronghold can store it forever without replenishment.\n\nThis sealing question ties to the opening “Blessed is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority.” Hands that hold authority hold water that flows. It also ties to the earlier test of best deeds; gratitude for flowing water is a test, using hearing, sight, intellect to thank, moving about regions to seek bounty while remembering often, and lending the good loan so provision does not merely circulate among the rich.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat water as mere metaphor for spirituality. It is literal provision whose sinking would be literal thirst, and the question is literal, yet it also illustrates spiritual flowing water of revelation that brings from darkness to light, the Light revealed in which belief is called.\n\nFor practice, consider this each time you drink, wash, or see a river. Ask who would bring flowing water if this sank, answer “The Most Compassionate alone,” believe and trust in Him, and translate that trust into protecting water, sharing it, and using provision to protect families from Fire and to walk upright, so the One who can make water sink will instead keep it flowing for you in this world and grant you its perfect, never-ceasing flow in Gardens of Eternity."
};